> What you describe is *exactly* what save scumming is, so it doesn't count.

No way.  Save scumming is using a save file to turn the clock
back in a game because you want to be able to try again.

If your system crashes and you use the save file as a backup,
the chances of system crashes per game or per hour of play are
so low that chances are you'd not use it.  There's a recover
utility that serves a backup function.  Using save files is a
belt and suspenders approach.

I tend to generate a save file when I finish the mines plus
Sokoban, then other after Medusa, another after the Castle,
another after Vlad, another at the VS, another just before
entering the planes.  But if I die in actual play I delete the
save file and start a new game.  I haven't lost a game to a
crash in about a year and the last time it happened the game
was early enough I didn't even bother trying "recover" on it.

For power outages - I play on my laptop now.  When I get a
warning that the battery is low I save the game, shutdown my
laptop and head for home.  Also most of the time I play at
home plugged in so the battery never does drain down.
Power outages never seem to outlast my batteries.  But when
playing at an espresso bar I don't push it.  As soon as I get
a battery warning I save and pack the laptop.


